What is Git Command Line: 
         The Git command line (CLI) is the interface where we execute Git commands using a terminal or shell.
         It works the same on: Windows (Git Bash / CMD)
                               Linux
                               macOS
                               Cloud environments
        GUI tools exist, but the command line provides full control and advanced features.
        Examples of GUI tools: GitHub Desktop, Visual Studio Code

Structure of a Git Command: 
Basic structure:
              git <command> <subcommand> <options>

How Git CLI Works Internally
When you run commands: Git reads .git directory
                       Updates objects (blob, tree, commit)
                       Updates branch pointer
                       Updates HEAD
Flow:
         Working Directory → Staging Area → Local Repo → Remote Repo

Importance of Command Line in DevOps: 
In DevOps: CI/CD pipelines run Git commands
           Servers don’t use GUI
           Automation scripts use CLI
           Debugging is easier
